<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Opt out of both cloud backup and device-to-device transfer.

  This is the file that actually matters, and the reason is a platform change
  that is easy to get wrong: from Android 12 (API 31) onwards,
  `android:allowBackup="false"` no longer disables *device-to-device* transfer.
  Cloud backup is off, but a direct device-to-device migration still copies
  `dataExtractionRules`-permitted data to the new device unless the rules
  exclude it. The previous contents of this file included `sharedpref` and
  `database` for cloud-backup and said nothing about device-transfer, so the
  configuration was simultaneously redundant with `allowBackup="false"` and
  silent about the case that was still live.

  What would have been transferred: the SQLCipher-encrypted message/channel
  database (unreadable elsewhere, since its passphrase is Keystore-wrapped and
  non-exportable -- so this is a disclosure of ciphertext, not plaintext), the
  `contacts.txt` list (which *is* plaintext), and the rolling diagnostic logs
  under `getExternalFilesDir`, which contain peer NodeIds and truncated MAC
  addresses.

  For an app whose entire premise is that its traffic and topology are not
  observable or exfiltrated, "the OS may quietly hand my contact list and peer
  identifiers to my new phone, and possibly to a backup provider" is not a
  defensible default. Excluding everything makes the intent explicit and
  reviewable.

  If backup is ever genuinely wanted, the right shape is a targeted allowlist
  for user-visible data only -- never `sharedpref` (identity seed and DB
  passphrase) and never `database`.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
